---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Main from "../components/Main.astro";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en" class="dark">
  <head>
    <BaseHead
      title="Projects · Patrik Simms"
      description="Things I am building on the side."
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <Header />
    <Main>
      <header class="mb-10 max-w-2xl">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Things I build</p>
        <h1 class="h1 mb-5">Projects</h1>
      </header>

      <article class="my-prose">
        <div class="not-prose flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
            <svg
              xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
              width="28"
              height="28"
              viewBox="0 0 64 64"
              aria-hidden="true"
              ><rect width="64" height="64" rx="14" fill="#93492b"></rect><path
                d="M23 11.5 h18 a4.5 4.5 0 0 1 4.5 4.5 v37 l-13.5 -8.8 -13.5 8.8 v-37 a4.5 4.5 0 0 1 4.5 -4.5 z"
                fill="#fcf6ea"></path><path
                d="M32 34.6 c-4.6 -3.3 -7.6 -6 -7.6 -9.6 a4.4 4.4 0 0 1 7.6 -3 a4.4 4.4 0 0 1 7.6 3 c0 3.6 -3 6.3 -7.6 9.6 z"
                fill="#93492b"></path></svg
            >
            <h2 class="h2">sakekeep</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <a
              href="https://sakekeep.app"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
              class="border-border bg-card hover:bg-accent text-card-foreground inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border px-3.5 py-1.5 text-sm font-medium transition-colors"
              >sakekeep.app →</a
            >
            <a
              href="https://github.com/patriksimms/sakekeep"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
              class="border-border bg-card hover:bg-accent text-card-foreground inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border px-3.5 py-1.5 text-sm font-medium transition-colors"
            >
              <svg
                xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
                width="16"
                height="16"
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="2"
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                aria-hidden="true"
                ><path
                  d="M15 22v-4a4.8 4.8 0 0 0-1-3.5c3 0 6-2 6-5.5.08-1.25-.27-2.48-1-3.5.28-1.15.28-2.35 0-3.5 0 0-1 0-3 1.5-2.64-.5-5.36-.5-8 0C6 2 5 2 5 2c-.3 1.15-.3 2.35 0 3.5A5.403 5.403 0 0 0 4 9c0 3.5 3 5.5 6 5.5-.39.49-.68 1.05-.85 1.65-.17.6-.22 1.23-.15 1.85v4"
                ></path><path d="M9 18c-4.51 2-5-2-7-2"></path></svg
              >
              GitHub
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground !mt-2 text-base">
          keepsake in reverse — a small present given to you by someone so that
          you will remember that person.
        </p>
        <p>
          I am very glad to work at a company where many coworkers have become
          friends. At some point a tradition emerged: instead of just collecting
          money for a farewell gift, we create something personal that reminds
          the person of the good, sad and defining moments of their time at the
          company. After organizing a lot of these farewell gifts, I was still
          dissatisfied with the tools available for creating them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Survey tools like Google or Microsoft Forms lost entries, didn't allow
          image uploads and made exporting tedious. Designing pages in
          PowerPoint, exporting them one by one and uploading them page by page
          to some online print-on-demand platform was frustrating and
          time-consuming.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a class="a" href="https://sakekeep.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
            >sakekeep</a
          > covers the whole chain: creating a survey, collecting
          people's contributions, designing page layouts and exporting
          everything print-ready.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="my-prose mt-16">
        <div class="not-prose flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
            <svg
              xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
              width="28"
              height="28"
              viewBox="0 0 32 32"
              aria-hidden="true"
              ><rect width="32" height="32" rx="6" fill="#171717"></rect><g
                fill="#fafafa"
                ><rect x="6" y="6" width="20" height="4" rx="1.5"></rect><rect
                  x="6"
                  y="14"
                  width="14"
                  height="4"
                  rx="1.5"></rect><rect x="6" y="22" width="8" height="4" rx="1.5"
                ></rect></g
              ></svg
            >
            <h2 class="h2">rundown</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <a
              href="https://rundown-app.dev"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
              class="border-border bg-card hover:bg-accent text-card-foreground inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border px-3.5 py-1.5 text-sm font-medium transition-colors"
              >rundown-app.dev →</a
            >
            <a
              href="https://github.com/patriksimms/rundown"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
              class="border-border bg-card hover:bg-accent text-card-foreground inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border px-3.5 py-1.5 text-sm font-medium transition-colors"
            >
              <svg
                xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
                width="16"
                height="16"
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="2"
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                aria-hidden="true"
                ><path
                  d="M15 22v-4a4.8 4.8 0 0 0-1-3.5c3 0 6-2 6-5.5.08-1.25-.27-2.48-1-3.5.28-1.15.28-2.35 0-3.5 0 0-1 0-3 1.5-2.64-.5-5.36-.5-8 0C6 2 5 2 5 2c-.3 1.15-.3 2.35 0 3.5A5.403 5.403 0 0 0 4 9c0 3.5 3 5.5 6 5.5-.39.49-.68 1.05-.85 1.65-.17.6-.22 1.23-.15 1.85v4"
                ></path><path d="M9 18c-4.51 2-5-2-7-2"></path></svg
              >
              GitHub
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground !mt-2 text-base">
          Agent-ready dashboard builder for marketing data.
        </p>
        <p>
          During my work as product engineer at esome I worked a lot on
          reporting dashboards to provide client data via a self-service
          interface to agencies and clients. During that time we experimented
          with a lot of different products, but were never satisfied with one
          solution. Most tools trade customizability for simplicity. This may
          work in simple contexts, but for very large and challenging clients
          with lots of requirements, most tools could not represent the
          complexity <em>or</em> were too technical for our internal account
          managers to use.
        </p>
        <p>
          Rundown tries to fill that gap by leveraging <a
            class="a"
            href="https://webmachinelearning.github.io/webmcp/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener">WebMCP</a
          > as a bridge for creating complex dashboards with simple instructions
          while keeping the flexibility of SQL under the hood. The unique angle
          of rundown is the combination of having a GUI for editing dashboards
          and seeing changes in real time while still offering tools for an
          agent interface like the ChatGPT app to be instructed by text.
        </p>
        <p>
          The project was created as a submission for the <a
            class="a"
            href="https://webmcp.devpost.com/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener">OpenAI WebMCP Challenge</a
          >. Currently it is invite only, but if you are interested in trying it
          out, <a
            class="a"
            href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/patrik-simms/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener">let me know</a
          >! The project is also open
          source under the MIT license on <a
            class="a"
            href="https://github.com/patriksimms/rundown"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener">GitHub</a
          >.
        </p>
      </article>
    </Main>
    <Footer />
  </body>
</html>
